                        "review": "A Great Game Ruined by an Awful, Unavoidable Soundtrack!\n\nI've wanted to play The Good Life for years, and I've only just managed to pick it up in a Steam sale for 85p. I was genuinely looking forward to playing it, but unfortunately, I simply cannot continue playing this game because of the awful, loud, whiny background music that you cannot turn down or switch off independently!\n\nAudio is half the enjoyment and experience of gaming. Good sound effects, combined with good visuals, are what make a game immersive.\n\nThe graphics are actually very good, and the game itself looks promising. However, the tutorial messages are ridiculously large, constantly obstructing the view. You quickly get sick of trying to see around them or through them just to enjoy the scenery and understand what's happening.\n\nBut the real game-breaking issue is THE MUSIC!\n\nThe soundtrack might be perfectly acceptable for a short burst in a promotional trailer, but when you're forced to listen to the same loud, whiny music for 20 or 30 minutes, it becomes absolutely unbearable.\n\nI'm not exaggerating when I say that my ears actually started hurting. I persevered because I really wanted to enjoy the game, but eventually I had to shut it down. My ears were still ringing 30 minutes after I'd stopped playing!\n\nAnd before anyone suggests the obvious, yes, I know I can turn the entire game's audio down or mute it through Windows. I've been gaming for many years, and I know how to adjust sound settings.\n\nTHAT IS NOT THE POINT!\n\nI don't want to turn off the engines, environmental sounds, water, weather and everything else that contributes to the atmosphere. I want to turn off the bloody background music!\n\nThat's why games have separate audio sliders for music, sound effects and overall volume. It's such a basic feature that I find it astonishing the developers apparently didn't think it necessary.\n\nWhat a waste of some genuinely good graphics and a promising game concept. Completely spoiled by the developers' own decisions.\n\nThese people don't need enemies. They've already shot themselves in the foot!\n\nThankfully, I only paid 85p for the game, and frankly, without the ability to control the background music independently, that's about all it's worth to me.\n\nHad I bought this game at its original full price, I would have been absolutely furious. In fact, it would probably have been the first game I've ever refunded, not because of the money, but simply to make a point about developers forcing an irritating soundtrack on players without giving them the option to disable it.\n\nI'm so glad I didn't pay full price.\n\nI'm sure there's a potentially great game here, but I'll probably never get to experience it properly because of one completely avoidable design decision.\n\nSteam recommendation: NOT RECOMMENDED.\n\nGive players separate music and sound-effect controls. It really isn't too much to ask.",
